Unsilon Production and Suppression as Measured by STAR in \(p+p\), \(d+\mathrm{Au}\), and

\(\mathrm{Au+Au}\) Collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=200\) GeV

Debye screening of inter-quark potentials leading to suppression of heavy quarkonia has long been a studied signature of the onset of partonic deconfinement. We report on the production of \(\Upsilon\)(1S+2S+3S) mesons at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=200\) GeV in \(p+p\), \(d+\mathrm{Au}\), and \(\mathrm{Au+Au}\) collisions as measured by the Solenoidal Tracker At RHIC. We compare our measured yield in \(p+p\) collisions to those in \(d+\mathrm{Au}\) and \(\mathrm{Au+Au}\) collisions to obtain nuclear modification factors and quantify suppression effects from both hot and cold nuclear matter. We separate our \(\mathrm{Au+Au}\) measurements into three centrality classes. We obtain a nuclear modification factor for \(\Upsilon\)(1S+2S+3S) in the rapidity range \(|y|<1\) in \(d+\mathrm{Au}\) collisions of\(R_{d\mathrm{Au}}=0.79\pm 0.24(\mathrm{stat.})\pm 0.03(\mathrm{sys.})\pm 0.10(p+p\) sys.). A comparison with models including shadowing and initial-state parton energy loss as well as final-state nuclear absorption indicates the presence of additional cold-nuclear matter suppression. In the top 10% most-central \(\mathrm{Au+Au}\) collisions, we measure a nuclear modification factor of \(R_{AA}=0.49\pm 0.1(\mathrm{stat.})\pm 0.02(\mathrm{sys.})\pm 0.06(p+p\) sys.), which is a larger suppression factor than that seen in cold nuclear matter. Our results are consistent with complete suppression of the excited \(\Upsilon\)(2S+3S) states and we calculate an upper limit of \(R_{AA}(\mathrm{2S+3S})<0.32\) with 95% confidence for the 0-60% centrality bin. The additional suppression see in \(\mathrm{Au+Au}\) collisions is consistent with model calculations using initial temperatures ranging from 330 to 442 MeV. However, a better understanding of the suppression seen in \(d+\mathrm{Au}\) collisions is needed before any definitive statements about suppression in \(\mathrm{Au+Au}\) collisions can be made.

