The BEMC has an energy resolution \(\sigma_{E}/E\) of \(\sim 14\%/\sqrt{E}\) plus a constant term of 1.5% which needs to be added in quadrature [23]. For a 5 GeV electron, this gives us a resolution of \(\sim\)6.5%. In \(A+A\) collisions, the high multiplicity of particles creates a background signal in the BEMC, leading to a deterioration of resolution of the measured energy. However, studies show that at an energy of 3 GeV, this effect only increases the resolution from 9% to 10% [23]. As this effect decreases with increasing energy, it is negligible for electrons coming from \(\Upsilon\) decays (\(>\sim 5\) GeV).

The BEMC is multipurpose in these analyses: its speed and coverage make it a useful trigger for identifying candidate \(\Upsilon\) events and it can be used in conjunction with the TPC to identify high-momentum electrons.

