For each dataset, we split the signal up into different kinematic regions for various reasons. In all cases, the signals are \(p_{T}\)-integrated. In the \(p+p\) system, the signal was split into two rapidity regions: \(|y|<0.5\) and \(0.5<|y|<1.0\) (see Fig. 6.17). The main purpose for this split is the shape of the background imposed by the L2 trigger. The mass cuts in the L2 trigger assume a vertex at \(V_{z}=0\) (see Tab. 6.1). Vertices displaced from the center of the TPC have increased acceptance of either forward or backward \(\Upsilon\), depending on the direction of the shift. As the reconstructed parent particle moves further away from \(y\approx 0\), the average vertex position shifts further and further away from \(V_{z}=0\). Hence, it made sense to split the \(p+p\)\(\Upsilon\) measurement into these two rapidity bins. This also allows us to report a rapidity spectrum (see Sec. 7.1).

The \(d+\mathrm{Au}\) signal was split into three regions: \(-1.0<y<-0.5\), \(|y|<0.5\), and \(0.5<y<1.0\) (see Fig. 6.18). Unlike in \(p+p\), we did not combine the forward and backward rapidity regions since the system is no longer symmetric in rapidity. As can be seen in Fig. 6.18, the Au-going direction (\(-1.0<y<-0.5\)) has a much larger Drell-Yan and correlated \(b\bar{b}\) decay background than the deuteron-going direction (\(0.5<y<1.0\)).

Since the \(\mathrm{Au+Au}\) dataset was collected with only the L0 trigger and not the L2 trigger, the mass turn-on is well below the \(\Upsilon\) mass (\(\sim 5.0\) GeV/\(c^{2}\)) and is not sensitive to the vertex position. This makes it possible to combine the dataset across rapidities when fitting to 

