### 7.2 Nuclear Modification Factor

To quantify the effect of nuclear matter on particle production, we calculate the nuclear modification factor (\(R_{AA}\), \(R_{d{\rm Au}}\)) defined as follows:

\[R_{AA}=\frac{1}{\frac{\sigma_{AA}}{\sigma_{pp}}}\times\frac{1}{\langle N_{\rm coll }\rangle}\times\frac{B_{ee}\times\left(\frac{d\sigma_{AA}}{dy}\right)^{\Upsilon} }{B_{ee}\times\left(\frac{d\sigma_{pp}}{dy}\right)^{\Upsilon}} \tag{7.1}\]

where \(\sigma_{xx}\) is the total nuclear inelastic cross section and \(\langle N_{\rm coll}\rangle\) is the average number of binary nucleon collisions as calculated by a Glauber model [130]. The first term in the equation controls for the relative chance to have any nuclear collision at all. The second term accounts for the number of nuclear collisions within a typical event. The third term is the actual ratio of production rates.

\(R_{AA}\) (or \(R_{d{\rm Au}}\) in the case of \(d+{\rm Au}\) collisions) is a very useful and easy-to-interpret quantity. When \(R_{AA}=1\), there is no nuclear modification at all; the number of particles produced in a typical event is the same as if \(<N_{\rm coll}>\) independent \(p+p\) collisions were overlaid. If \(R_{AA}=0\), no particles are produced-often referred to as complete suppression. If \(R_{AA}>1\), nuclear effects have enhanced the yield of particles.

Using the 2009 \(p+p\) results as the baseline, we calculated \(R_{AA}\) and \(R_{d{\rm Au}}\) for multiple states, rapidities, and centralities. The nuclear modification factors were calculated using the following total inelastic cross sections: \(\sigma_{pp}=42\) mb, \(\sigma_{d{\rm Au}}=2.2\) b, and \(\sigma_{{\rm AuAu}}=6\) b. The results are summarized in Tab. 7.2. The reported uncertainties on our measured \(R_{AA}\) and \(R_{d{\rm Au}}\) values are fourfold: (1) uncertainty from statistics and fitting from the \(d+{\rm Au}\) or \({\rm Au+Au}\) cross section; (2) statistical and fitting uncertainty from \(p+p\); (3) \(d+{\rm Au}\) or \({\rm Au+Au}\) systematics which do not cancel with the \(p+p\) measurement and are not joint to both the \(d+{\rm Au}\) and \({\rm Au+Au}\) measurements; and (4) systematic uncertainties from the \(p+p\) measurement which do not cancel with the \(d+{\rm Au}\) or \({\rm Au+Au}\) measurement along with systematics joint to both the \(d+{\rm Au}\) and \({\rm Au+Au}\) measurements.

We show the \(R_{d\rm Au}\) results for both PHENIX (green diamonds) and STAR (red stars) as a function of rapidity in Fig. 7.4. The RHIC results are compared to CEM predictions using EPS09 NLO shadowing predictions [37] (green band). The width of the band at highly negative rapidities is dominated by uncertainty in the magnitude of the Gluon EMC effect. Elsewhere, the bulk of the uncertainty comes from the range of individual EPS09 parameterizations. Note that this prediction actually predicts and enhancement on the order of \(R_{d\rm Au}\approx 1.1\) at midrapidity whereas our results show a suppression of \(R_{d\rm Au}=0.48\pm 0.16\ ({\rm stat.})\pm 0.06\ ({\rm syst.})\). Similar calculations using other nPDFs (EKS90, EPS09, and nDSg) also find an enhancement at midrapidity on the order of 5-20% [137].

